Summary of Logical Relationships  
─────────────────────────────

- **G1 → G2 and G3 [AND, Sequential]:**  
  The top-level goal (G1) is decomposed into a sequential collection phase (G2) followed by a delivery phase (G3). Both must succeed for the mission to be complete.

- **G1 → G1.1 [OR, Parallel]:**  
  An alternative execution strategy is provided via G1.1, where collection and delivery tasks are executed concurrently across multiple storages and delivery locations.

- **G2 → AT1, AT2, AT3 [AND] with Fallback AT4:**  
  The collection phase (G2) is executed by sequentially performing AT1 (navigate), AT2 (request), and AT3 (wait). If the robot’s battery falls below 10%, AT4 (recharge and reassign) is triggered as a fallback.

- **G3 → AT5, AT6, AT7 [AND] with Fallback AT8:**  
  The delivery phase (G3) is executed by sequentially performing AT5 (navigate), AT6 (deliver), and AT7 (repeat delivery if needed). If the battery falls below 30%, AT8 (return to checkpoint and reassign) is triggered as a fallback.

- **G3 → G4 [AND] (Failure Handling):**  
  If the fallback in delivery (AT8) fails (i.e. the resource is not returned to the checkpoint), G4 is activated to handle the failure by triggering an alert (AT9) and sending a report (AT10).

- **G1.1 → AT11 and AT12 [AND, Parallel]:**  
  In the parallel execution alternative (G1.1), tasks AT11 and AT12 are executed concurrently to handle multiple items.

